# Where the backend lives.
#
# Leave EMPTY in development. The client then uses "/api", which vite proxies to
# localhost:4100 — the browser sees one origin, so CORS never applies locally.
#
# In production set this to the deployed backend's ORIGIN, e.g.
# "https://api.example.com". Requests then become cross-origin, which is the
# point at which enableCors() on the backend must be narrowed to this app's
# origin (it currently allows everything).
VITE_API_URL=""

# Where the app QR code sends a customer's phone (§309) — and the "Get the app"
# advert. Leave EMPTY until the customer app is published: in development the
# code then opens the app in Expo Go on this computer's Wi-Fi (exp://…:8081),
# found by itself when the dev server starts.
#
# Once published, set ONE link we control that sends each phone to its own
# store (e.g. "https://bwangureservations.rw/app"), not a store's address:
# shops print this code, and printed cards must keep working whatever changes
# behind the link.
VITE_CUSTOMER_APP_URL=""
